e need to be making  some subtle change that is making the  dining experience better and so  fail-fast sounds really scary when you  look at it on a big picture and that's  something we don't do we take great  steps to mitigate against that big  picture risk but on a small scale we're  always looking to make improvements  whether it's a garnish or a presentation  or trying to sell it or a slight change  in our steps a service or a new menu  presentation I want our organization to  always be willing to make those little  changes so one of the great things about  the restaurant business it's one of the  few businesses that has such a tight  feedback loop I serve you a salad I'm  gonna know within the first couple bites  of that salad is proper temperature is  served at the proper time if the flavor  profiles right I can know very  immediately from your facial reaction  frankly I know that quality is not easy  and yet I think quality is ultimately a  differentiator and I'm not looking for  the easy fixes I'm looking for the  quality I'm looking for things that are  going to be attractive to a specific  palate specific consumer and to me  that's a defining piece of advice in a  defining element of how I make decisions  as an entrepreneur and for me and I want  to be in a position where I'm creating  new things and that's speaking to my own  motivations and I think it's really  important to express those needs and  desires rather than just be a slave if  you will to what the expectations are  I'm passionate about the food and in a  family business I think it's okay to be  explicit about what your motivations are  and what you're passionate about think  in the end that's going to make you more  comfortable more confident and being in  this role for a career I'm not going  anywhere right  I'm not looking to jump in two years  when the next best offer comes around  I'm looking at the next 30 to 40 years
 

